2026-10-20T14:42:02.913590Z TRACE tungstenite::protocol: Received message {"type": "response.output_item.done", "sequence_number": 393, "output_index": 1, "item": {"type": "custom_tool_call", "id": "ctc_07f6106a40a44a50016ad77db8170c819898af275f659c5b09", "metadata": {"turn_id": "01a159c2-ec01-7091-975d-1eefdc2d9b88"}, "internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough": {"create_time": 1792507320.09124, "turn_id": "01a159c2-ec01-7091-975d-1eefdc2d9b88"}, "call_id": "call_87f42b42654641c88634b95a098eabe7", "name": "exec", "input": "const essay = `At dawn, the river woke before the town. Mist hovered above its dark surface, turning every bend into a secret. Beneath the haze, water slipped over stones, carrying leaves, reflections, and the quiet memory of distant hills. A heron stood near the bank, patient as a statue, waiting for breakfast to reveal itself.\n\nBy midmorning, sunlight scattered across the current. Children arrived with jars, searching for minnows in shallow pools. Their laughter startled the heron into flight, and its broad wings briefly joined the river\u2019s moving pattern. On the bridge, commuters hurried past, rarely noticing how the water continued its ancient work below them.\n\nThe river had no single personality. In spring, swollen by rain, it pushed against its banks and rearranged fallen branches. During summer, it narrowed and warmed, inviting swimmers to its familiar bends. Autumn filled it with copper leaves, while winter hardened its edges and sharpened the air. Yet through every season, it remained both traveler and teacher, leaving one place while belonging to none.\n\nAt sunset, an old fisherman lowered a line from the muddy shore. He had visited the river for decades, not because every evening brought a catch, but because waiting beside the current made time feel honest. The river never promised stillness. It offered motion, change, and the chance to begin again farther downstream.\n\nWhen night covered the valley, the town quieted. Under the stars, the river became a silver thread, winding toward an unseen horizon. It carried no speeches, yet it connected farms, forests, houses, and people who might never meet. Listening to its steady voice, I understood that a river is more than water traveling toward the sea. It is a living passage, gathering stories as it goes, and reminding everyone near its banks that movement can be a form of belonging.`;\nnotify(String(essay.trim().split(/\\s+/).length));\n", "status": "completed"}}
