k movements of trout. By sunrise, light spills across the water, and the current carries leaves toward the valley. The river seems patient, yet it never pauses. Each bend changes its voice, from a whisper among reeds to a deep, steady conversation with the banks. Villages have grown beside this river for generations. Farmers draw water for fields, children learn to swim in its warm shallows, and elders sit beneath cottonwood trees, watching seasons pass. Bridges cross it at narrow places, joining roads, families, and stories. In winter, ice gathers along the edges. In spring, snowmelt swells the channel, testing every wall and footpath. Floods can frighten people, but they also spread rich soil across the floodplain, preparing gardens for another year. Farther downstream, the river enters a crowded city. Concrete replaces mud, and engines replace birdsong, but the current remains recognizable. It reflects towers, carries rain through hidden channels, and reminds hurried walkers that the landscape still has a pulse. When citizens restore wetlands and remove litter, the river answers with clearer water and returning herons. At sunset, the river turns copper beneath the open sky. It has traveled farther than any person can see, gathering streams, memories, and the marks of every place it touched. Yet its destination is not an ending. At the coast, freshwater mingles with salt, and the river becomes part of a larger motion. Watching it, I understand that movement does not mean leaving everything behind. Its waters teach resilience, patience, and belonging, binding mountain rain to ocean, present footsteps to distant beginnings. The river carries its past forward, reshaping the world quietly, one measured current at a time.' | wc -w\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":1000});\ntext(r.output);", "status": "completed"}], "error": null, "incomplete_details": null, "tool_choice": "auto", "parallel_tool_calls": false, "prompt_cache_key": "01a159c2-d110-7ad2-8199-6